- op: discuss  # d351
  at: '2026-08-24T18:40:20Z'  # d351
  by: agent  # d351
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d351
  clock: 3  # d351
  nonce: d351  # d351
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-08-24, in their words: \"yes — WI-lutuh is the long pole and\nneeds scoping now; items 1-3 are prerequisites that make its output readable\nbut don't substitute for it.\"\n\nWHAT THIS SETTLES, and it is a scope decision the project had never actually\nmade: VERIFY-CLAIMS IS MEANT TO BE USABLE ON NON-PYTHON REPOSITORIES. The\nquestion put to the owner was whether that is a near-term goal or whether the\nRCT is a python substrate and the enumeration audit can wait. It is a goal, so\nthis item is no longer a P2 rust footnote — it is the long pole for the whole\nfeature outside python.\n\nTHE SIZE, measured before the ruling: only python.yaml declares any\n`module_completeness` at all. FOURTEEN of the fifteen catalogued languages\ndeclare ZERO — bash, c, cpp, elixir, erlang, go, haskell, java, javascript,\nkotlin, objc, rust, scala, swift. Step (2) of _uncatalogued_external_modules\n(\"an unmatched call is an examined negative ONLY over an enumerated module\")\ncan therefore never pass for any of them, so a clean boundary verdict is\nreachable only when EVERY external call happens to classify. Filed originally\nas a rust-only gap; it is not.\n\nRAISED TO P1 ON THE STRENGTH OF THE RULING. The agent is not re-ranking this on\nits own judgement — the owner named it the long pole and asked for it to be\nscoped now.\n\nWHAT \"SCOPING\" HAS TO PRODUCE, since the work is an AUDIT and not code: for\neach language, how many distinct stdlib modules a representative corpus\nactually reaches (the real denominator, not the size of the stdlib), what\nevidence a dated `module_completeness` entry requires per module, and which\nlanguages pay off first. python.yaml's 76 entries and\ndocs/surveys/python-stdlib-module-io-enumeration.md are the worked precedent\nfor both the format and the evidence bar.\n\nSEQUENCING THE OWNER SET: INV-juvul, then INV-rilit, then WI-nakut, then this.\nThose three are PREREQUISITES AND NOT SUBSTITUTES — the owner said so\nexplicitly. Measured basis: on express 7 of 17 and on bellman 6 of 23 reported\n\"unexamined modules\" are first-party (INV-juvul) and 4 of bellman's 23 are one\nmodule counted under two spellings (INV-rilit). Removing all of that still\nleaves both repos `inconclusive`, because what remains is stdlib — which is\nthis item."  # d351
- op: discuss  # de0f
  at: '2026-08-24T23:23:01Z'  # de0f
  by: agent  # de0f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # de0f
  clock: 4  # de0f
  nonce: de0f  # de0f
  message: "SCOPED 2026-08-24, as the owner asked -- AN AUDIT, NOT CODE. Full document:\ndocs/surveys/stdlib-module-completeness-scope.md. Evidence, sample, sweep and\nscoring scripts: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lutuh_scope_08242026/\n\nMETHOD. 475 corpus repos classified by top-level manifest; a language-stratified\nsample of 55 run through the shipped verify-claims on merged dev (@dfe3ba8b3e);\neach repo's uncatalogued list recomputed from its own survey using the gate's\nOWN functions rather than a reimplementation. 42 of 55 produced a survey -- 12\nhit the 420s timeout, 1 was killed. THE EXCLUDED SET IS THE LARGEST REPOS, which\nreach MORE modules, so EVERY COUNT BELOW IS A LOWER BOUND.\n\n(1) THE DENOMINATOR -- tens of modules per language, not thousands. Distinct\nstdlib modules the sample REACHES:\n\n  objc 127+  haskell 69+  go 53  python 53  erlang 38+  java 38+  rust 33\n  elixir 32+  cpp 29  javascript 13  scala 13+  swift 2+\n  (+ = classifier unreliable for that language, see below)\n\nRUST -- the language this item was filed about -- REACHES 33. That is a\ntractable audit, comparable to python's 27-in-one-sitting.\n\n(2) THE EVIDENCE BAR. Five rules, extracted from python.yaml's 76 entries and\nthe python survey: total-surface (every surface the module exposes, not the\nfunctions this repo calls); EXACT matching (urllib does not vouch for\nurllib.request); the file-OBJECT rule (json.load(fp) is not I/O, gzip.open is);\nthe vocabulary traps (logging covers stdout/stderr so PRINTING is I/O; env_read\ncovers os.environ); and refuse-by-default, because the wrong answer is a false\nall-clear.\n\nTHE BAR IS NOT MET BY READING, and this is the number that drives (3): the\npython audit's first draft was written from knowledge of the modules and FIVE OF\nITS 32 VERDICTS WERE WRONG -- pathlib, typing (reveal_type -> stderr), base64\n(main opens files), shlex (reads sys.stdin), contextlib (chdir). A 16% error\nrate for expert reading unaided, on the auditor's best language, with EVERY\nerror on the false-all-clear side. A mechanical probe caught them -- and that\nprobe is python-specific (importlib + inspect.getsource) and DOES NOT PORT.\nLocally available today: python (done), javascript (node builtinModules), rust\n(rust-src IS installed), c/cpp (headers). NOT available: go, java, kotlin,\nswift, scala, haskell, elixir, erlang, objc. objc is the extreme case -- its\nstdlib is Foundation/Cocoa, which does not exist on this platform at all.\n\n(3) WHICH LANGUAGES PAY FIRST -- and a finding that reframes the question.\n\nqualifying_only is `not unknown` (verified in code at verify_claims.py:2960,\nnot from a docstring), so the list must reach EXACTLY ZERO. And io_primitives\nstays STDLIB-SCOPED by standing ruling, so any THIRD-PARTY entry needs a\nper-project overlay too. Whole-repo flippability, 42 repos:\n\n  28  NEEDS_OVERLAY_TOO (third-party present)\n  10  BLOCKED_BY_ARTIFACT (producer defect first)\n   2  ALREADY_ZERO\n   2  STDLIB_ONLY -- WI-lutuh alone flips it\n\nRestricted to the four languages with a reliable classifier (10 eligible\nrepos): 7 need an overlay, 2 already zero, 1 stdlib-only.\n\nSO: COMPLETING THE STDLIB AUDIT FOR EVERY LANGUAGE WOULD TAKE ROUGHLY 1 CORPUS\nREPO IN 10 TO A CLEAN VERDICT. This item is NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT; the\ndominant blocker is third-party coverage, which the shipped catalogue cannot\naddress by design.\n\nTHE CONTROL THAT PROVES IT, and it is the strongest thing in the audit: PYTHON\nIS THE ONE LANGUAGE WHOSE AUDIT IS DONE (76 entries). If enumeration were\nsufficient, python repos would be at zero. Across 16 sampled repos python\ncontributes 243 uncatalogued entries -- 75 stdlib, 168 THIRD-PARTY -- and only\n4 of 16 are stdlib-only, all four repos where python is incidental (1-9\nentries). The real python projects are third-party-dominated: h5py 27, polis 54,\nvmaf 37. And 53 distinct stdlib modules remain unenumerated after the 76-entry\naudit, several of them the slot-mismatch family the python survey already filed\nas a DEFECT rather than catalogue work (sys.stdout.buffer, io.StringIO,\nqueue.Queue, threading.Event, typing.Dict).\n\nPARTIAL WORK PAYS ZERO -- the curve makes the knife-edge visible:\n  python  K=5:0/4  K=10:2/4  K=20:3/4  K=53:4/4\n  rust    K=5:0/1  K=10:0/1  K=20:1/1  K=33:1/1\n  erlang  K=5:0/1  K=10:0/1  K=20:1/1  K=38:1/1\nrust and erlang buy NOTHING at K=10 and everything at K=20-33.\n\nRECOMMENDED ORDER (a recommendation with its inputs shown, not a decision):\n  1. rust        83 corpus repos,  33 modules, bar meetable (rust-src local)\n  2. javascript  92 corpus repos,  13 modules, bar meetable (node builtins)\n  3. python      36 corpus repos,  53 left, long tail + slot-mismatch defects\n  4. go          88 corpus repos,  53 modules, BAR NOT MEETABLE (no toolchain)\n  --  cpp        BLOCKED by INV-zimud (filed today). Do not start here.\n  --  java/swift/objc/scala  DECIDE FIRST: the module slot carries bare TYPE\n      names (System, Bundle, NSData) and even syntax (swift $0, already filed\n      as INV-kotob). module_completeness may be the wrong instrument there.\n\nWHAT THE AUDIT DOES NOT DO: it does not decide the order; it does not cost the\nthird-party/overlay path (28 repos counted, not costed); it does not re-audit\npython's 76 entries; and its denominators are lower bounds.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN NUMBERS, made before publishing: my Go classifier\nsplit on \".\" before \"/\", truncating github.com/x to \"github\" and reporting every\nthird-party Go module as stdlib (151/0 -> 53/97). And a module reached by two\nlanguages (vmaf reaches `math` from cpp AND python) was credited to whichever\nwrote last, which put python's ALREADY-ENUMERATED `math` on python's list; the\ngate's own `unknown` is a set[str] with no language attached, so attribution is\na reconstruction -- 71 of 3,905 entries (1.8%) are multi-language."  # de0f
- op: discuss  # 2077
  at: '2026-08-26T01:09:43Z'  # 2077
  by: agent  # 2077
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2077
  clock: 5  # 2077
  nonce: '2077'  # 2077
  message: "DECISION RECORDED BY THE AGENT, not the owner -- reversible, reasoning below. This settles campaign decision point 3 (\"the instrument decision for java/swift/objc/scala completeness\").\n\nTHE QUESTION AS THE PLAN FRAMED IT: those four languages \"await an OWNER instrument decision (their module slot carries bare TYPE names, so module_completeness may be the wrong instrument).\"\n\nDECISION: module_completeness is NOT the wrong instrument. The MODULE SLOT is wrong, and that is a producer defect with its own filed item. Keep one instrument; fix the slot.\n\nWHY. INV-kotob (violated) already establishes the mechanism for Swift: the analyzer emits the receiver VARIABLE NAME into the module slot, so the hint is a local name rather than a module. WI-gudak carries the fix (the Swift analyzer stamps the receiver expression into ExternalRef.module_path). That is a producer bug, not evidence that a module-keyed completeness declaration is the wrong shape of instrument.\n\nBuilding a second, type-keyed completeness instrument for four languages would put ONE FACT IN TWO HOMES -- a module-keyed declaration for nine languages and a type-keyed one for four -- with the more permissive one silently winning wherever both could apply. That is the failure mode the project has paid for repeatedly. It would also require new axis scaffolding under ADR-0024 for a distinction that exists only because an analyzer writes the wrong string.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE QUEUE: java/swift/objc/scala stay OUT of WI-lutuh, but they are now blocked on a NAMED producer fix rather than on an open design question. Swift's is INV-kotob / WI-gudak. The rust -> javascript -> python order is unaffected and stands (see the P3 re-rank: WI-lutuh governs DISCLOSURE, and measurement 0006 measures PRECISION, so 0006 gives no reason to re-order it).\n\nHONEST GAP, do not skip this before queueing the other three. I confirmed the module-slot defect for SWIFT only. The plan asserts java/objc/scala also carry bare type names in the module slot, and I could find NO filed item for any of them -- searching the open corpus for module-slot defects returns swift (INV-kotob, WI-gudak), c++ (INV-zimud), python (INV-mumov) and go (WI-kamin), and nothing for java, objc or scala. So the plan's premise for three of the four languages is UNVERIFIED. Confirm or refute it per language before queueing them; if it turns out java's module slot is fine, java simply joins the normal WI-lutuh queue and this whole decision point never applied to it."  # 2077
- op: discuss  # 0e99
  at: '2026-08-26T16:34:00Z'  # 0e99
  by: agent  # 0e99
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0e99
  clock: 6  # 0e99
  nonce: '0e99'  # 0e99
  message: "DO NOT START THE RUST AUDIT YET. Measured on merged dev bd638f8cbf 2026-08-26,\nwhile beginning it. The 33-module denominator is CORRECT and reproduces exactly;\nwhat is wrong is the assumption that enumerating them changes a verdict.\n\nI BUILT THE PROBE AND THEN CHECKED WHETHER ITS OUTPUT COULD PAY. It cannot, on\neither rust repo I can test.\n\nTHE MECHANISM IS A LADDER, and this is the whole finding. `boundary_coverage`\n(verify_claims.py ~3195-3245) is a sequence of EARLY RETURNS:\n\n  1. languages with no I/O catalogue at all\n  2. supported languages that produced no call edges\n  3. method_starved_modules            <- catalogued method-shaped, no method edge\n  4. opaque launch sites\n  5. UNCATALOGUED MODULES              <- THIS ITEM, and it is LAST\n\nBoth testable rust repos return at a rung ABOVE this item, so the uncatalogued\nlist is never consulted:\n\n  encrypted-dns-server  (rust+toml only)   returns at rung 3 --\n      \"catalogued I/O is method-shaped (std::fs::File, std::path::Path) but\n       produced no method call edge for any of them\"\n  ripgrep               (rust+fish+toml)   returns at rung 1 --\n      \"language(s) with no taint catalogue (fish, toml)\"\n\nI RECORD A WRONG INTERMEDIATE CLAIM RATHER THAN QUIETLY DROPPING IT, because it\nis the kind that would have been believed: I first observed that the string\n\"uncatalogued\"/\"never examined\" appears ZERO times in both the text and JSON\noutput for encrypted-dns-server, and concluded the blocker was GONE -- drained\nby INV-pusin (#504) and INV-hukuf (#546). THAT WAS WRONG. Recomputing\n`_uncatalogued_external_modules` DIRECTLY on each repo's own cached survey,\nbypassing the ladder, the lists are undiminished:\n\n  encrypted-dns-server   32 uncatalogued, 16 rust stdlib\n  ripgrep                25 uncatalogued, 17 rust stdlib\n\nBoth reproduce the 2026-08-24 scoping lists module for module. The blocker is\nnot gone; it is SHORT-CIRCUITED, and an absent string is exactly what a\nshort-circuit looks like from outside. The evidence for \"gone\" and the evidence\nfor \"outranked\" are identical at the output, which is why I went to the function.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS ITEM'S OWN K-CURVE. The curve (\"rust K=20:1/1, K=33:1/1\")\npredicts one eligible rust repo flips at K=20. That is a statement about the\nuncatalogued LIST reaching zero, not about the VERDICT changing, and the two are\nnot the same claim once a higher rung fires. On the two repos I can test,\ncompleting all 33 changes neither verdict. The curve is not wrong; it measures a\ndifferent quantity than \"flips the repo\", and this item reads it as the latter.\n\nAND INV-linub ALREADY SAYS THIS, which is why I am not treating it as a\nre-ranking on my own judgement. Its filed rule: \"Completeness and reachability\nMULTIPLY ... So L2/L3 is sequenced first and L1 follows.\" Its exposure table\nlists rust at 53% method-kind and its L2 status as NOT MEASURED. I have now\nmeasured it (note filed there). Rust is the second worked instance of the rule\nkotlin supplied the argument for.\n\nWHAT I RECOMMEND, AND WHAT I AM NOT DECIDING. The rust L1 audit is not\nworthless -- it is necessary and it is genuinely blocked behind rung 3. But\ndoing it now buys zero measurable change, and the same effort at rung 3 (rust's\nL2) unblocks it. Reordering the campaign is the owner's call, so this note\nrecords the evidence and the item stays P1/todo_soft, unchanged.\n\nWORK BANKED, so none of it is lost if the order stands:\n  ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/lutuh_rust_08262026/  -- probe.py + modules.txt +\n  probe.json. A mechanical rust-src I/O probe (the Python audit's importlib\n  probe does not port; this is its analogue). Over the 33 modules: 10 CANDIDATE,\n  23 REFUSE, refuse-by-default, and it never grants -- a candidate still needs\n  adjudication because a re-export can carry I/O in from a module whose own text\n  is clean.\n\n  IT CAUGHT THE PATHLIB TRAP, which is the reason it exists: std::path::Path\n  REFUSED on canonicalize/metadata/read_dir. Path DOES do filesystem I/O, and\n  `pathlib` is exactly the verdict the Python audit's first draft got wrong.\n\n  TWO OF MY OWN BUGS, BOTH FALSE-ALL-CLEAR GENERATORS, found and fixed before\n  trusting any output: (a) a directory module resolved to `mod.rs` ALONE because\n  the is_file() test preceded is_dir(), so std::collections was adjudicated on a\n  file that is mostly re-exports and reported NO SIGNAL -- three verdicts flipped\n  to REFUSE once fixed; (b) bare `std` resolved to lib.rs alone and came back\n  CANDIDATE, the single most dangerous false positive available here, since\n  marking `std` complete would turn every unmatched std call into an examined\n  negative. Both were caught by disbelieving an implausible file count, not by\n  the probe reporting an error.\n\n  ADJUDICATION NOTES for whoever resumes: std::ptr and std::slice are FALSE\n  refusals (they match on `ptr::metadata`, pointer metadata, not fs metadata).\n  std::sync / Arc / Mutex refuse on `sys::sync` = futex/condvar, which is not any\n  of the nine boundary kinds (fs_read/fs_write/net_send/net_recv/subprocess/\n  env_read/host_info_read/ipc_recv/logging) and is arguably grantable.\n  std::sync::mpsc is INTRA-process, so not ipc_recv -- a vocabulary call, not a\n  reading of the source. std::time/Duration/SystemTime refuse because a clock\n  read maps to no boundary kind cleanly; refuse-by-default applies."  # 0e99
